window.TOPICS=window.TOPICS||{};window.TOPICS['kmeans']={"id": "kmeans", "title": "K-means clustering", "tagline": "Lloyd's algorithm, objective, K-means++. 29 PYQs (97 marks) solved step by step.", "concepts": {"calc": {"name": "Computing K-means by hand", "marks": 9, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Do it as a table. Columns: point, squared distance to each centre, chosen cluster. Fill the whole table before touching the centres. Then one row per cluster: its points and their mean. Repeat until the cluster column does not change, and only then compute F from the final table.</p>"}, "update": {"name": "The update step (means)", "marks": 12, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Picture the centre as the balance point of its cluster: the mean is the single spot that makes the total squared distance smallest. So after every assignment, replace each centre by the plain average of its own points, coordinate by coordinate.</p>"}, "assign": {"name": "The assignment step (nearest centre)", "marks": 19, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Each point asks one question: which centre is closest? Compare squared distances (no square roots needed, the order is the same). A point never looks at other points, only at the centres, and the centres stay fixed while you assign.</p>"}, "objective": {"name": "Objective F, monotonicity, convergence", "marks": 25, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>F is the total squared distance from points to their own centre. Assigning to the nearest centre can only lower F, and moving a centre to its mean can only lower F, so F never goes up. But many different end states are possible, so the result is a local minimum that depends on where you started.</p>"}, "kpp": {"name": "K-means++ initialisation", "marks": 32, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>K-means++ spreads the starting centres out. After the first random centre, every point gets a weight equal to its squared distance to the nearest chosen centre, and the next centre is drawn in proportion to these weights. Far points are likely, chosen points have weight 0.</p>"}}, "sections": [{"id": "orient", "title": "Orientation: clustering and K-means", "html": "<p>Clustering splits unlabeled points into groups of similar points. K-means does it with a fixed number $K$ of groups, each represented by a centre (its mean).</p><div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Where this sits</div><p>K-means is the first clustering method. It uses hard assignments (each point in exactly one cluster). GMM with EM, later, is its soft-assignment cousin.</p></div><h3>You will be able to</h3><ol><li>Compute the K-means objective for given clusters.</li><li>Run assign and update steps by hand.</li><li>Explain why Lloyd's algorithm stops, and why it can stop at a poor answer.</li><li>Do the K-means++ seeding probabilities.</li></ol><h3>Prerequisite check</h3><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Squared distance of $(1,2)$ and $(4,6)$</summary><p>$9+16=25$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Mean of $1,2,6$</summary><p>$3$.</p></details><h3>PYQ sources</h3><p>This topic was converted from the Quiz 1 notes. The full teaching (intuition, worked examples, widgets) stays on the Quiz 1 pages; every PYQ below is solved here in the Quiz 2 format with each option explained.</p><ul><li><a href=\"#q1-b17\">4.1 to 4.3: the K-means algorithm and the update/assign steps</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b18\">4.4: objective, monotonicity and convergence</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b19\">4.4 (continued): objective calculations</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b20\">4.5: K-means++ initialisation (part A)</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b21\">4.5: K-means++ initialisation (part B)</a></li></ul><p>A few Quiz 1 PYQs could not be rebuilt because their data or figure is missing from the source copy; they stay on the Quiz 1 pages only.</p><p>Flagged questions (marked [FLAG] or [?]) have a key that disagrees with the printed data, or no key in the source. Both readings are shown.</p>"}, {"id": "obj", "title": "The objective and the two steps", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Objective</div>$$F=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{c(i)}\\|^2$$<p>$x_i$ are the points, $c(i)$ the cluster of point $i$, $\\mu_k$ the centre of cluster $k$. $F$ is the total squared distance of points to their own centre; smaller is tighter.</p></div><div class=\"box analogy\"><div class=\"bt\">Everyday analogy</div><p>Choosing $K$ meeting points for a group of friends, so that the total squared walking distance is small. Each friend walks to the nearest meeting point (assign), then each meeting point moves to the centre of the friends who chose it (update). <b>Where the analogy stops:</b> squared distance punishes one very far friend much more than plain distance would.</p></div><h3>Lloyd's algorithm</h3><ol><li><b>Initialise</b> $K$ centres.</li><li><b>Assign</b> each point to its nearest centre.</li><li><b>Update</b> each centre to the mean of its assigned points.</li><li>Repeat 2 and 3 until assignments stop changing.</li></ol><p><b>Why the mean?</b> For a fixed cluster, $\\sum\\|x_i-\\mu\\|^2$ is smallest when $\\mu$ is the mean. <b>Why it stops:</b> the assign step cannot increase $F$ (each point picks its nearest centre) and the update step cannot increase $F$ (the mean minimises it); $F\\ge0$ and the number of assignments is finite, so it converges, but only to a <i>local</i> minimum.</p><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 1 (simple, 1-D)</div><p>Points $1,2,6,7$, initial centres $1$ and $2$. Assign: $1\\to c_1$; $2,6,7\\to c_2$. Update: $\\mu_1=1$, $\\mu_2=(2+6+7)/3=5$. Assign again: $2$ is $1$ from $\\mu_1$ and $3$ from $\\mu_2$, so it moves to cluster 1: $\\{1,2\\},\\{6,7\\}$. Update: $\\mu=1.5,\\ 6.5$. Assignments no longer change. $F=4\\times0.25=1.0$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 2 (exam level, 2-D)</div><p>Points $(0,0),(0,2),(4,0),(4,2)$, centres $(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$. Assign: $(0,0),(0,2)\\to$ centre 1; $(4,0),(4,2)\\to$ centre 2. Update: $(0,1)$ and $(4,1)$. $F=4\\times1=4$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 3 (tricky): $F$ never increases</div><p>If you ever compute a larger $F$ after a full round, there is an arithmetic slip. Also: $K=n$ gives $F=0$ (every point its own centre), and $K=1$ gives the total variance. $F$ always decreases as $K$ grows, so it cannot be used by itself to choose $K$.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Using distance, not squared distance, in $F$.</li><li>Updating centres before all points are assigned.</li><li>Believing the final answer is the global optimum.</li><li>Forgetting that an empty cluster has no mean (keep the old centre or re-seed).</li></ul></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 5 (tricky): a tie and an empty cluster</div><p>Points $0,4$ with centres $2$ and $10$. Point $0$: distances² $4$ and $100$. Point $4$: $4$ and $36$. Both go to centre $2$, so the centre at $10$ gets no points. Its mean is undefined: the usual fix is to keep the old centre (or re-seed it at the point farthest from its centre). If a point is exactly halfway between two centres, follow the rule in the question (usually the lower-numbered centre).</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 6 (exam level): 2-D, two rounds</div><p>Points $P=(1,1),Q=(2,1),R=(4,3),S=(5,4)$, centres $(0,0),(4,4)$. Round 1, assign: $P$: $2$ vs $18$ (cluster 1); $Q$: $5$ vs $13$ (1); $R$: $25$ vs $1$ (2); $S$: $41$ vs $1$ (2). Update: $\\mu_1=(1.5,1)$, $\\mu_2=(4.5,3.5)$. Round 2, assignments unchanged, so it has converged. $F=0.25+0.25+0.5+0.5=1.5$.</p></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Step through Lloyd's algorithm on the points of worked example 6. Watch $F$ after every assign and every update: it never increases.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"kmstep\" data-points=\"1,1;2,1;4,3;5,4\" data-init=\"0,0;4,4\"></div></div></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Each coloured region is the set of locations whose nearest centre is that centre. Click anywhere to probe it.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"vcells\" data-centres=\"0,0;4,4\" data-points=\"1,1;2,1;4,3;5,4\" data-box=\"-1,-1,7,7\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "kq1", "concept": "assign", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Centres $(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$. Which cluster does $(1,3)$ join?", "options": [{"t": "Centre $(0,0)$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: $1+9=10<9+9=18$."}, {"t": "Centre $(4,0)$", "ok": false, "why": "$18>10$."}, {"t": "Both, it is a tie", "ok": false, "why": "$10\\ne18$."}, {"t": "Neither", "ok": false, "why": "Every point is assigned."}]}, {"id": "kq2", "concept": "assign", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "1-D points $0,3,4,10$; centres $2$ and $9$. How many points join centre $9$?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "Only $10$: $3$ and $4$ are closer to $2$ ($1$ and $4$ versus $36$ and $25$).", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq3", "concept": "calc", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $1,2,6,7$; initial centres $6$ and $7$. After the first assign and update, what is the smaller centre?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "Assign: $1,2,6\\to6$; $7\\to7$. Update: $(1+2+6)/3=3$ and $7$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq4", "concept": "calc", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $0,2,10,12$; initial centres $0$ and $2$. $F$ after convergence?", "num": {"ans": 4, "tol": 0.001, "show": "4"}, "explain": "Round 1: $\\{0\\},\\{2,10,12\\}$, centres $0,8$. Round 2: $\\{0,2\\},\\{10,12\\}$, centres $1,11$. Stable. $F=1+1+1+1=4$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq5", "concept": "update", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Why is each centre moved to the mean of its points?", "options": [{"t": "The mean minimises the sum of squared distances to those points", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "The mean is the median", "ok": false, "why": "Different statistics."}, {"t": "It makes clusters equal in size", "ok": false, "why": "Sizes are not balanced."}, {"t": "It guarantees the global optimum", "ok": false, "why": "Only a local optimum is guaranteed."}]}]}, {"id": "init", "title": "Initialisation and K-means++", "html": "<p>Different starting centres can give different final clusters and different $F$. Bad starts need more iterations and can end in worse local minima.</p><div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">K-means++ seeding</div><ol><li>Pick the first centre uniformly at random from the points.</li><li>For each point $x$ compute $D(x)^2$, its squared distance to the nearest centre chosen so far.</li><li>Pick the next centre with probability $D(x)^2/\\sum_yD(y)^2$.</li><li>Repeat until $K$ centres are chosen.</li></ol></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 4</div><p>Points $(0,0),(0,2),(4,0),(4,2)$, first centre $(0,0)$. $D^2=0,4,16,20$ (total $40$). Probabilities for the second centre: $0,\\ 0.1,\\ 0.4,\\ 0.5$. A far point is likelier, so the centres tend to spread out; the point already chosen has probability $0$.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Using $D(x)$ instead of $D(x)^2$ as the weight.</li><li>Thinking K-means++ guarantees the global optimum (it only makes bad starts unlikely).</li><li>Letting an already-chosen point have non-zero probability.</li></ul></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>K-means++ seeding: circle area shows the probability of each point being picked next. Click a point to pick it and see the probabilities update.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"kmpp\" data-points=\"0,0;1,1;1,-1;8,0;9,1;9,-1;5,8\" data-start=\"0\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "kq6", "concept": "kpp", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "1-D: first centre $0$; other points $1,3,4$. Probability that K-means++ picks $4$ next? (3 decimals)", "num": {"ans": 0.615, "tol": 0.002, "show": "0.615"}, "explain": "$D^2=1,9,16$, total $26$; $16/26=0.615$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq7", "concept": "kpp", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Centres $0$ and $10$ already chosen; remaining points $2$ and $7$. Probability of picking $7$? (3 decimals)", "num": {"ans": 0.692, "tol": 0.002, "show": "0.692"}, "explain": "$D^2(2)=4$; $D^2(7)=\\min(49,9)=9$; $9/13=0.692$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq8", "concept": "kpp", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Can K-means++ pick an already chosen centre again?", "options": [{"t": "No, its weight $D^2$ is $0$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "Yes, with probability $1/n$", "ok": false, "why": "Weights are not uniform."}, {"t": "Yes, if it is far from the others", "ok": false, "why": "Its own distance is $0$."}, {"t": "Only in the last round", "ok": false, "why": "Never."}]}]}, {"id": "pyqs", "title": "All PYQs of this topic, solved", "kind": "pyq", "html": ""}, {"id": "rev", "title": "Revision page", "html": "<h3>Formulas</h3><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Formula</th></tr><tr><td>Objective</td><td>$F=\\sum\\|x_i-\\mu_{c(i)}\\|^2$</td></tr><tr><td>Update</td><td>$\\mu_k=\\tfrac1{|S_k|}\\sum_{i\\in S_k}x_i$</td></tr><tr><td>K-means++</td><td>$P(x)=D(x)^2/\\sum D^2$</td></tr></table><h3>Exam decision flow</h3><ol><li>Assign by squared distance (ties: follow the question).</li><li>Update to means.</li><li>Stop when assignments repeat; compute $F$ from the final clusters.</li></ol><h3>True/false facts</h3><ul><li>Converges: yes. Global optimum: not guaranteed.</li><li>Result depends on initialisation and on $K$.</li><li>$F$ decreases with $K$.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "test", "title": "Topic test: mixed questions", "kind": "test", "html": ""}, {"id": "checklist", "title": "Checklist: skills you should be able to do", "kind": "checklist", "html": ""}], "pyqs": [{"id": "km01", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 5, "type": "SA", "concept": "calc", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "4.1 Core K-means calculations and properties", "tagNote": "Source accepts 6. The printed table has four wrong cells; with the corrected table, $F$ right after assignment is 10 and after the centre update is 6. Reading 6 as \"after one full update\" is our inference [?].", "wording": "Consider the data points: $(-1,0),(-1,1),(-1,-1),(2,0),(3,1),(3,-1),(4,0)$. K-means is initialised with $k=2$: $\\mu_1^0=(-1,0),\\ \\mu_2^0=(2,0)$. Squared distances to the initial means are:<table><tr><th>Point</th><th>$\\|x_i-\\mu_1^0\\|^2$</th><th>$\\|x_i-\\mu_2^0\\|^2$</th></tr><tr><td>$(-1,0)$</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>$(-1,1)$</td><td>1</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>$(-1,-1)$</td><td>1</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>$(2,0)$</td><td>3</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>$(3,1)$</td><td>17</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>$(3,-1)$</td><td>17</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>$(4,0)$</td><td>5</td><td>2</td></tr></table>After assigning every point to the closest cluster, find the objective $F(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}\\|^2$.", "key": {"lo": 6, "hi": 6, "show": "6"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the objective $F$ after assigning each point to its closest cluster, from 7 points, $k=2$ and a printed distance table.</p>", "idea": "<p>$F$ needs both arguments: the labels $z$ and the centres $\\mu$. Recompute any suspicious table cell with $\\|x-\\mu\\|^2$ before trusting it.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Recompute the table. Four printed cells are wrong: $(-1,0)$ to $\\mu_2^0$ is $9$ (printed 3); $(2,0)$ to $\\mu_1^0$ is $9$ (printed 3); $(4,0)$ to $\\mu_1^0$ is $25$ (printed 5); $(4,0)$ to $\\mu_2^0$ is $4$ (printed 2). The other 10 cells are right.</li><li>Assign by row minimum: $z=(1,1,1,2,2,2,2)$. Cluster 1 $=\\{(-1,0),(-1,1),(-1,-1)\\}$, cluster 2 $=\\{(2,0),(3,1),(3,-1),(4,0)\\}$. (The four errors do not change any assignment.)</li><li>With the old centres, $F(z,\\mu^0)=0+1+1+0+2+2+4=10$. The printed table would give $8$.</li><li>Update the centres: $\\mu_1^1=\\tfrac13[(-1,0)+(-1,1)+(-1,-1)]=(-1,0)$ and $\\mu_2^1=\\tfrac14[(2,0)+(3,1)+(3,-1)+(4,0)]=(3,0)$.</li><li>With the updated centres: cluster 1 gives $0+1+1=2$ and cluster 2 gives $1+1+1+1=4$, so $F(z,\\mu^1)=6$.</li><li>The accepted answer $6$ is therefore $F$ after one full assign-and-update, not the value immediately after assigning (which is $10$).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Shortcut for the updated $F$: each cluster costs $\\sum\\|x\\|^2-n\\|\\mu\\|^2$. Cluster 1: $5-3\\cdot1=2$. Cluster 2: $40-4\\cdot9=4$. Total $6$. Gap identity: $F(z,\\mu^0)=F(z,\\mu^1)+\\sum_kn_k\\|\\mu_k^0-\\mu_k^1\\|^2=6+3\\cdot0+4\\cdot1=10$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code check: $z=(1,1,1,2,2,2,2)$; $F(z,\\mu^0)=10$; $\\mu^1=((-1,0),(3,0))$; $F(z,\\mu^1)=6$; the next pass changes nothing. The gap $10-6=4=4\\cdot\\|(2,0)-(3,0)\\|^2$ matches.</p>", "trap": "Writing $8$ straight from the printed table (the table is wrong), or writing $10$ because \"after assigning\" sounds like the old centres. The accepted answer is $6$; reading it as \"after one full update\" is our inference [?].", "rule": "<p>When $F$ appears, write both arguments. In a printed distance table, recompute at least one cell where the point is not on the same side as its centre.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(2,0),(0,2),(6,6),(8,6),(6,8)$ with $\\mu^0=(0,0),(6,6)$. Find $F(z,\\mu^0)$ and $F(z,\\mu^1)$.", "a": "$z=(1,1,1,2,2,2)$. $F(z,\\mu^0)=16$. $\\mu^1=(\\tfrac23,\\tfrac23),(\\tfrac{20}3,\\tfrac{20}3)$ and $F(z,\\mu^1)=32/3\\approx10.667$. Gap: $16-\\tfrac{32}3=\\tfrac{16}3$ ✓."}}}, {"id": "km02", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "objective", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "4.1 Core K-means calculations and properties", "tagNote": "Printed notation error: (1) and (3) are identical in this copy. Reading them as the three terms of the Lloyd chain makes the source key (option 3) consistent. This is an inference [?].", "wording": "For Lloyd's algorithm, what would be the correct relationship among the following three quantities?<br>$(1)\\ \\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}^{t+1}\\|^2\\qquad(2)\\ \\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}^{t}\\|^2\\qquad(3)\\ \\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}^{t+1}\\|^2$<br>The paper defines $\\mu_{z_i}^t$ and $\\mu_{z_i}^{t+1}$ as the means of cluster $z_i$ at iterations $t$ and $t+1$, and $\\mu_{z_i}^{t+1}$ corresponds to the cluster to which $x_i$ will move in the next iteration.", "options": ["(1) > (2) > (3)", "(1) < (2) < (3)", "(3) < (1) < (2)", "(2) < (3) < (1)"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the correct order of three objective values within one Lloyd iteration.</p>", "idea": "<p>Assigning points to the nearest centre never raises $F$, and replacing each centre by its cluster mean never raises $F$. So $F$ falls (or stays) along $(z^t,\\mu^t)\\to(z^{t+1},\\mu^t)\\to(z^{t+1},\\mu^{t+1})$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Read the three quantities as pairs $(z,\\mu)$: $(2)=F(z^t,\\mu^t)$, $(1)=F(z^{t+1},\\mu^t)$, $(3)=F(z^{t+1},\\mu^{t+1})$. (As printed, $(1)$ and $(3)$ look identical; this reading is our reconstruction [?].)</li><li>Assign step ($(2)\\to(1)$): centres $\\mu^t$ are fixed. Each point moves to its nearest centre, so its cost cannot rise: $(1)\\le(2)$.</li><li>Update step ($(1)\\to(3)$): labels $z^{t+1}$ are fixed. A cluster mean minimises the cluster\\'s sum of squares, so $(3)\\le(1)$.</li><li>Chain: $(3)\\le(1)\\le(2)$. With a genuine change the inequalities are strict, giving option 3.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Numeric witness on data $\\{0,2,4,9,10\\}$, second pass: $(2)=22.667$, $(1)=18.222$, $(3)=8.5$, so $(3)<(1)<(2)$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: on 30 random points with 3 centres, every pass satisfied $F(z^t,\\mu^t)\\ge F(z^{t+1},\\mu^t)\\ge F(z^{t+1},\\mu^{t+1})$ (first pass $37.507\\ge36.040\\ge34.643$).</p>", "trap": "Rejecting the question because $(1)$ and $(3)$ print the same, or reading the chain backwards. At convergence the \"<\" become \"$=$\"; the MCQ means a generic step.", "rule": "<p>Three quantities with $t$ and $t+1$: write each as a pair $(z,\\mu)$; only one argument changes per step; $F$ goes down.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "In one iteration $F(z^t,\\mu^t)=50$ and $F(z^{t+1},\\mu^t)=44$. Can $F(z^{t+1},\\mu^{t+1})=45$? How small can $F(z^t,\\mu^{t+1})$ be?", "a": "No: $F(z^{t+1},\\mu^{t+1})\\le44$. And $\\mu^t$ is already the mean of $z^t$, so $F(z^t,\\mu)\\ge50$ for every $\\mu$; hence $F(z^t,\\mu^{t+1})\\ge50$."}, "optWhy": ["Says the assign step raised $F$. False.", "Says the newest value $(3)$ is the largest. False: it is the smallest.", "<b>Correct.</b> This is the Lloyd chain $(3)<(1)<(2)$.", "Says the oldest value $(2)$ is the smallest. False."]}}, {"id": "km03", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "calc", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.1 Core K-means calculations and properties", "wording": "Let $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Define $v^*=\\arg\\min_{v\\in\\mathbb R^d}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\|3x_i-2v\\|^2$. Which is the correct expression for $v^*$?", "options": ["$v^*=\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i$", "$v^*=\\dfrac32\\cdot\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i$", "$v^*=\\dfrac3n\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i$", "$v^*=\\dfrac23\\cdot\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the minimiser $v^*$ of $\\sum\\|3x_i-2v\\|^2$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The squared-error sum $\\sum\\|x_i-v\\|^2$ is minimised by the mean. Here the scalars $3$ and $2$ rescale the target: $\\arg\\min\\sum\\|ax_i-bv\\|^2=\\tfrac ab\\bar x$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Let $f(v)=\\sum_i\\|3x_i-2v\\|^2$. Then $\\nabla f=\\sum_i2(3x_i-2v)(-2)=-4\\sum_i(3x_i-2v)$.</li><li>Set $\\nabla f=0$: $\\sum_i3x_i=2nv$, so $v=\\tfrac{3}{2n}\\sum_ix_i=\\tfrac32\\bar x$.</li><li>The Hessian is $8nI\\succ0$, so this is the minimum. Option 2.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Substitution: $\\|3x_i-2v\\|^2=4\\|v-\\tfrac32x_i\\|^2$, so $v^*$ is the mean of the $y_i=\\tfrac32x_i$, i.e. $\\tfrac32\\bar x$. Quick test: $n=1$, $x_1=1$ gives $(3-2v)^2$, minimised at $v=1.5$; only option 2 gives $1.5$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: for random $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^3$, a numerical minimiser equals $1.5\\cdot\\bar x$ in all three coordinates.</p>", "trap": "Inverting the ratio. Check by size: $2v\\approx3x$ means $v\\approx\\tfrac32x$.", "rule": "<p>$\\arg\\min_v\\sum\\|ax_i-bv\\|^2=\\tfrac ab\\times$ mean.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Write $v^*=\\arg\\min_v\\sum_i\\|4x_i+2v\\|^2$ in terms of the mean $\\bar x$.", "a": "$4x_i+2v=4x_i-(-2)v$, so $a=4,b=-2$ and $v^*=\\tfrac ab\\bar x=-2\\bar x$."}, "optWhy": ["Ignores the scalars: that is the minimiser of $\\sum\\|x_i-v\\|^2$.", "<b>Correct.</b> $v^*=\\tfrac32\\cdot\\tfrac1n\\sum x_i$.", "Forgets to divide by $n$ and uses $3$ instead of $\\tfrac32$.", "Flips the ratio ($\\tfrac ba$ instead of $\\tfrac ab$)."]}}, {"id": "km04", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "update", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.2 Centroid updates and iteration calculations", "tagNote": "\"The provided formula\" is not printed in this copy; the standard centroid formula is used [?].", "wording": "Suppose you have a dataset of 9 two-dimensional data points and are performing K-means clustering with $K=3$. After the first iteration: $C_1=\\{(2,2),(3,3),(4,4)\\}$, $C_2=\\{(6,6),(7,7),(8,8)\\}$, $C_3=\\{(10,10),(11,11),(12,12)\\}$. After the second iteration, which option represents the updated cluster centroid $\\mu_1$ for $C_1$ using the provided formula?", "options": ["$\\mu_1=(2.5,2.5)$", "$\\mu_1=(4.5,4.5)$", "$\\mu_1=(3,3)$", "$\\mu_1=(5.5,5.5)$"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the updated centroid $\\mu_1$ of cluster $C_1$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The updated centroid is the mean of the cluster\\'s current members: $\\mu_1=\\tfrac1{|C_1|}\\sum_{x\\in C_1}x$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>After iteration 1: $\\mu_1=\\bigl(\\tfrac{2+3+4}3,\\tfrac{2+3+4}3\\bigr)=(3,3)$, $\\mu_2=(7,7)$, $\\mu_3=(11,11)$.</li><li>Iteration 2 assignment: $(4,4)$ is closest to $(3,3)$; $(6,6)$ and $(8,8)$ are closest to $(7,7)$; $(10,10)$ is closest to $(11,11)$. No point changes cluster.</li><li>Membership is unchanged, so $\\mu_1=(3,3)$. Option 3.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The points $(2,2),(3,3),(4,4)$ are equally spaced, so their mean is the middle point $(3,3)$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: mean $(3,3)$; the second assignment pass gives the same membership; matches the source answer.</p>", "trap": "Moving $\\mu$ further because it is \"the second iteration\". If no point changes cluster, the update returns the same mean. Another slip is averaging all nine points.", "rule": "<p>\"Updated centroid of $C_k$\" means the mean of the cluster\\'s current members.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Find the centroid of $\\{(1,2),(3,4),(5,9)\\}$. If $(5,9)$ is replaced by $(7,3)$, what is the new centroid?", "a": "First $(3,5)$. Then $\\{(1,2),(3,4),(7,3)\\}$ gives $(\\tfrac{11}3,3)\\approx(3.667,3)$."}, "optWhy": ["Mean of only $\\{2,3\\}$: a point was left out.", "Mean of a wrong subset: not the members of $C_1$.", "<b>Correct.</b> The mean of $2,3,4$ is $3$.", "Mean of a wrong subset or a mix with $C_2$."]}}, {"id": "km05", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "update", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.2 Centroid updates and iteration calculations", "tagNote": "The wording does not say which centres the update starts from. Both readings (add the point to the converged centres, or re-run from the initial centres) give the same answer [?].", "context": "Data points: $(-2,2),(-2,-2),(0,0),(2,2),(2,-2),(8,2),(8,-2),(9,1),(9,-1)$. K-means with $k=2$; the converged centres for this data are $(0,0)$ and $(8.5,0)$. (The plotted figure of the original question is not reproduced here.)", "wording": "After introducing a new data point $(7,0)$, the cluster centers were updated. Enter the sum of the updated x-coordinates for both cluster centers accurately, rounding your answer to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 8.18, "hi": 8.22, "show": "8.18 to 8.22"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the sum $x_1^{new}+x_2^{new}$ of the x-coordinates of the two updated centres.</p>", "idea": "<p>A new point joins its nearest cluster; only that cluster\\'s mean moves: $\\mu_{new}=\\mu+\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{n+1}$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Cluster sums of the x-coordinates: cluster 1 (the five points around $(0,0)$) has $-2-2+0+2+2=0$; cluster 2 has $8+8+9+9=34$ with $4$ points.</li><li>The new point $(7,0)$ is much closer to cluster 2 (squared distances about $1$ or $2.25$ versus $49$), so cluster 2 now has $5$ points and x-sum $34+7=41$.</li><li>Cluster 2 centre: $41/5=8.2$. Cluster 1 centre: $0$.</li><li>Sum: $0+8.2=8.2$. The accepted range is $8.18$ to $8.22$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Incremental mean from the converged centre $8.5$ (4 points): $8.5+\\tfrac{7-8.5}5=8.2$. A full Lloyd re-run from $(0,0),(6,0)$ on all 10 points also ends at $(0,0)$ and $(8.2,0)$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: both readings (add the point to converged centres; re-run from the initial centres) give $(0,0),(8.2,0)$, so the sum is $8.2$.</p>", "trap": "Giving the new point to the wrong cluster, or treating both centres as changed (cluster 1 did not move). Note the sum equals $8.2$ here only because cluster 1\\'s x-centre is $0$.", "rule": "<p>New point: find its nearest cluster, then update only that cluster\\'s mean with $\\mu+\\frac{x-\\mu}{n+1}$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(2,0),(0,2),(2,2)$ and $(10,0),(12,0),(10,2),(12,2)$ with centres $(1,1)$ and $(11,1)$. A new point $(7,1)$ arrives. Sum of the updated centres\\' x-coordinates?", "a": "$(7,1)$ is closer to $(11,1)$ ($16$ vs $36$). $\\mu_2^{new}=(11,1)+\\tfrac{(7,1)-(11,1)}5=(10.2,1)$; $\\mu_1=(1,1)$. Sum $=11.2$."}}}, {"id": "km06", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q14", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "update", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.2 Centroid updates and iteration calculations", "context": "Six points in $\\mathbb R^2$: $(-1,-4),(5,6),(-2,-2),(4,5),(-3,-3),(3,4)$. K-means with $k=2$, initial means $(-4,-4)$ and $(6,6)$.", "wording": "If $(x_1,y_1)$ is the mean of the first cluster after convergence, find $x_1+y_1$.", "key": {"lo": -5, "hi": -5, "show": "-5"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the converged mean of cluster 1 and the sum of its coordinates.</p>", "idea": "<p>Lloyd\\'s algorithm alternates assignment and mean update until no assignment changes.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Pass 1 assignment (squared distances to $(-4,-4)$ and $(6,6)$): $(-1,-4)$: $(9,149)\\to1$; $(5,6)$: $(181,1)\\to2$; $(-2,-2)$: $(8,128)\\to1$; $(4,5)$: $(145,5)\\to2$; $(-3,-3)$: $(2,162)\\to1$; $(3,4)$: $(113,13)\\to2$.</li><li>Update: $C_1=\\{(-1,-4),(-2,-2),(-3,-3)\\}$ gives $\\mu_1=(-2,-3)$; $C_2=\\{(5,6),(4,5),(3,4)\\}$ gives $\\mu_2=(4,5)$.</li><li>Pass 2 assignment (to $(-2,-3)$ and $(4,5)$): $(-1,-4)$: $(2,106)\\to1$; $(5,6)$: $(130,2)\\to2$; $(-2,-2)$: $(1,85)\\to1$; $(4,5)$: $(100,0)\\to2$; $(-3,-3)$: $(1,113)\\to1$; $(3,4)$: $(74,2)\\to2$. Nothing changes, so the algorithm has converged.</li><li>$\\mu_1=(-2,-3)$, so $x_1+y_1=-5$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The data forms two clear groups (three negative points, three positive points). Guess those as the clusters, compute the means, and confirm the fixed point with the pass-2 table.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: means $(-2,-3)$ and $(4,5)$, converged after one update; final $F=8$; $x_1+y_1=-5$ matches the key.</p>", "trap": "Writing the initial centre\\'s sum ($-8$), or stopping after pass 1 without the verification pass. Watch the signs: $-2-3=-5$.", "rule": "<p>\"After convergence\": assign, update, then verify that a further pass changes nothing.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(3,-6),(3,-4),(2,-2),(-3,6),(0,5),(-4,0)$ with $k=2$ and initial centres $(-2,-1)$ and $(-6,2)$. Cluster 1\\'s converged $x_1+y_1$?", "a": "Pass 1: only $(-3,6)$ goes to cluster 2; means $(0.8,-1.4)$ and $(-3,6)$. Pass 2: $(0,5)$ moves to cluster 2, giving $\\mu_1=(1,-3)$ and $\\mu_2=(-1.5,5.5)$, and pass 3 changes nothing. $x_1+y_1=-2$."}}}, {"id": "km07", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "update", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.2 Centroid updates and iteration calculations", "context": "Six points in $\\mathbb R^2$: $(-1,-4),(5,6),(-2,-2),(4,5),(-3,-3),(3,4)$. K-means with $k=2$, initial means $(-4,-4)$ and $(6,6)$.", "wording": "If $(x_2,y_2)$ is the mean of the second cluster after convergence, find $x_2+y_2$.", "key": {"lo": 9, "hi": 9, "show": "9"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the converged mean of cluster 2 and the sum of its coordinates, from the same run as the previous question.</p>", "idea": "<p>Both sub-questions share one run: the fixed point found there has $C_2=\\{(5,6),(4,5),(3,4)\\}$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>From the converged run, $C_2=\\{(5,6),(4,5),(3,4)\\}$ (in both passes).</li><li>$\\mu_2=\\bigl(\\tfrac{5+4+3}3,\\tfrac{6+5+4}3\\bigr)=(4,5)$.</li><li>$x_2+y_2=4+5=9$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Sum trick: $x_2+y_2=\\tfrac13\\sum_{i\\in C_2}(x_i+y_i)=\\tfrac{11+9+7}3=9$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $\\mu_2=(4,5)$, sum $9$, matching the key. Together with the previous question the answers $-5$ and $9$ both match.</p>", "trap": "Writing the initial centre $(6,6)$\\'s sum ($12$), or averaging a wrong subset.", "rule": "<p>Children of one comprehension share one run: run it once, then read off each answer.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "In the previous question\\'s transfer data, what is cluster 2\\'s converged $x_2+y_2$?", "a": "The converged $\\mu_2=(-1.5,5.5)$, so $x_2+y_2=4$."}}}, {"id": "km08", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "objective", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.2 Centroid updates and iteration calculations", "tagNote": "Only two statements are visible in this copy; the original may have more. Option 2 is the source key [?].", "wording": "Which of the following statements is always true for the K-means algorithm with $K<n$ cluster centres, between consecutive iterations $t$ and $t+1$?", "options": ["If a data point changes its cluster assignment from cluster $j$ to cluster $k$, it is guaranteed that the centroid of cluster $j$ strictly changes.", "It is not possible for the K-means algorithm to revisit a configuration, where a configuration corresponds to the $K$-way partition generated by clustering at the end of each iteration."], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statement is true for every run, from the two statements visible in this copy.</p>", "idea": "<p>$F$ never increases (Lloyd chain) and there are finitely many partitions, so a partition cannot repeat after the algorithm has moved away from it. But a point changing cluster does not force a centroid to move.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Statement 1 (a point moves from $j$ to $k$, so centroid $j$ strictly changes). Counterexample in 1-D: $x=\\{-1,2,2,3,6\\}$, $K=2$, $z^t=(1,2,2,1,2)$ gives $\\mu^t=(1,\\tfrac{10}3)$.</li><li>Reassign: $z^{t+1}=(1,1,1,2,2)$, so the point $3$ moves from cluster 1 to cluster 2. But $\\mu_1^{t+1}=\\tfrac{-1+2+2}3=1=\\mu_1^t$: centroid 1 did not change. Statement 1 is not always true.</li><li>Statement 2 (a configuration is never revisited). Each partition has an associated cost $F$ with its own means. Every real change of partition strictly lowers $F$ (with a fixed tie-break rule), so the costs form a strictly decreasing sequence while the partition keeps changing.</li><li>If a partition returned to an earlier one, $F$ would return to an earlier value, impossible for a strictly decreasing sequence. Statement 2 is always true. Option 2.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>On the data $\\{0,2,4,9,10\\}$ the partitions go $(1,1,2,2,2)\\to(1,1,1,2,2)\\to(1,1,1,2,2)$ with $F$: $22.667\\to8.5\\to8.5$. The only repeat is the converged one that has stopped, never a return to an old partition.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code reproduced the counterexample: $z^{t+1}=(1,1,1,2,2)$, $\\mu^{t+1}=(1,4.5)$, $\\mu_1^t=\\mu_1^{t+1}=1$; a random 1-D search found more such cases.</p>", "trap": "Believing \"a point moved, so the centroid moved\". Also confusing \"no revisit\" with \"global optimum\" (K-means can end in a local minimum). Statement 2 assumes a fixed tie-break rule.", "rule": "<p>For \"always true\" about K-means: monotone $F$ and finiteness give true statements; \"strictly\", \"guaranteed\", \"global optimum\" wording is usually false.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Two consecutive iterations have equal $F$, yet some point changed cluster. What must have happened?", "a": "A tie: that point was at equal distance from two centres, and the tie-break moved it. With a \"move only if strictly closer\" rule, equal $F$ means no point moved."}, "optWhy": ["False: a point can leave cluster $j$ and the mean of $j$ can stay the same (counterexample above).", "<b>True.</b> $F$ decreases strictly on every genuine change, so no partition can reappear."]}}, {"id": "km09", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "assign", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "4.3 Cluster assignment, distance, and geometry", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key is derived by running Lloyd on the data.", "wording": "Consider the following Hard K-means clustering problem with four points: $P(1,1),\\ Q(2,1),\\ R(4,3),\\ S(5,4)$. Consider the number of clusters to be $k=2$ and the initial centroids to be $C_1=(0,0),\\ C_2=(4,4)$. After how many iterations will the algorithm terminate and what will be the final centroids? Use Euclidean distance metric to calculate distance.", "options": ["$2,\\ (1,1),\\ (4,4)$", "$3,\\ (1,1),\\ (4,4)$", "$2,\\ (1.5,1),\\ (4.5,3.5)$", "$4,\\ (1.5,1),\\ (4.5,3.5)$"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the number of iterations until termination and the final centroids.</p>", "idea": "<p>Run Lloyd: assign, update, and stop after the pass in which no assignment changes. Final centroids are the means of the final clusters.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Pass 1 (centres $(0,0),(4,4)$): squared distances $P:(2,18)$, $Q:(5,13)$, $R:(25,1)$, $S:(41,1)$, so $z=(1,1,2,2)$.</li><li>Update: $C_1=\\{P,Q\\}$ gives $(1.5,1)$; $C_2=\\{R,S\\}$ gives $(4.5,3.5)$.</li><li>Pass 2 (centres $(1.5,1),(4.5,3.5)$): $P:(0.25,18.5)$, $Q:(0.25,12.5)$, $R:(10.25,0.5)$, $S:(21.25,0.5)$, so $z=(1,1,2,2)$ again. Nothing changed: terminate.</li><li>Two iterations (counting the terminating pass); final centroids $(1.5,1)$ and $(4.5,3.5)$. Option 3.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Bisector test: the pass-1 boundary is $x_1+x_2\\le4$ (cluster 1), and the pass-2 boundary is $3x_1+2.5x_2\\le14.625$; both give $z=(1,1,2,2)$. Elimination: a final centroid must be the mean of a non-empty subset, and no subset of these points has mean $(4,4)$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: Lloyd stops after 2 passes with means $(1.5,1),(4.5,3.5)$; $F=0.5+1=1.5$.</p>", "trap": "Not counting the terminating pass (giving 1), or being attracted by options that keep $(4,4)$. The counting convention (terminating pass counts) is a convention [?].", "rule": "<p>\"Terminate\" means count the pass in which nothing changes; check that proposed final centroids can be subset means.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(1,1),(2,0),(5,4),(6,5)$, $k=2$, initial centres $(0,0)$ and $(6,6)$. Number of iterations and final centroids?", "a": "Pass 1: $z=(1,1,1,2,2)$; update gives $(1,\\tfrac13)$ and $(5.5,4.5)$. Pass 2 changes nothing: 2 iterations, final $(1,\\tfrac13),(5.5,4.5)$."}, "optWhy": ["Contains $(4,4)$, which is not the mean of any subset of the points, so it cannot be a final centroid.", "Same problem: $(4,4)$ and $(1,1)$ are not means of final clusters.", "<b>Correct.</b> Two iterations, centroids $(1.5,1)$ and $(4.5,3.5)$.", "The centroids are right, but pass 2 already changes nothing, so it is not 4 iterations."]}}, {"id": "km10", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "assign", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.3 Cluster assignment, distance, and geometry", "tagNote": "Answer indicated in the source: C1.", "wording": "Consider 9 data points: $(2,3),(1,2),(3,1),(5,6),(7,8),(9,9),(6,7),(10,10),(11,11)$. Suppose we aim to form 3 clusters using the K-means algorithm. After the first iteration: $C_1=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}$; $C_2=\\{(2,3),(5,6),(6,7)\\}$; $C_3=\\{(9,9),(7,8),(10,10),(11,11)\\}$. After the second iteration, to which cluster will the data point $(2,3)$ be reassigned?", "options": ["C1", "C2", "C3", "It remains in the same cluster as in the first iteration"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the new cluster of the point $(2,3)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>First turn the clusters into centres (means), then send the point to the nearest centre.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Centres: $\\mu_1=(\\tfrac{1+3}2,\\tfrac{2+1}2)=(2,1.5)$; $\\mu_2=(\\tfrac{13}3,\\tfrac{16}3)$; $\\mu_3=(9.25,9.5)$.</li><li>Squared distances from $(2,3)$: to $\\mu_1$: $0+2.25=2.25$; to $\\mu_2$: $(\\tfrac73)^2+(\\tfrac73)^2=\\tfrac{98}9\\approx10.89$; to $\\mu_3$: $52.5625+42.25=94.8125$.</li><li>The smallest is $2.25$, so $(2,3)$ goes to $C_1$. Option 1.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Bisector between $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$: $2x^T(\\mu_2-\\mu_1)\\le\\|\\mu_2\\|^2-\\|\\mu_1\\|^2$ gives $\\tfrac{97}3\\le\\tfrac{1475}{36}$, true, so $\\mu_1$ is nearer. $C_3$ is far away.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: distances $(2.25,10.889,94.8125)$; after the assignment $z=(1,1,1,2,3,3,2,3,3)$, with $(2,3)$ in $C_1$.</p>", "trap": "Using the nearest data point instead of the centre (here it happens to agree, but the method is wrong), or mixing the point into $\\mu_2$ before moving it.", "rule": "<p>\"Clusters given, reassign a point\": compute all means first, then one set of squared distances.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Seven points $(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(6,6),(7,6),(6,7),(3,2)$ with $C_1=\\{(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\\}$, $C_2=\\{(6,6),(7,6),(6,7),(3,2)\\}$. Where does $(3,2)$ go in the next iteration?", "a": "$\\mu_1=(\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13)$, $\\mu_2=(5.5,5.25)$. Distances: $\\tfrac{89}9\\approx9.89$ to $\\mu_1$ and $16.81$ to $\\mu_2$, so it goes to $C_1$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b> $(2,3)$ is closest to $\\mu_1$.", "It is currently in $C_2$, but $\\mu_2$ is farther ($10.89$ vs $2.25$).", "$\\mu_3$ is far away ($94.8$).", "It does move: its nearest centre is not $\\mu_2$."]}}, {"id": "km11", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "assign", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.3 Cluster assignment, distance, and geometry", "wording": "K-means clustering is run on a dataset of 100 points with $K=3$. If the points $\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$ are part of the same cluster $C_1$, which of the following points will definitely lie in $C_1$?", "options": ["$\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}$"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which point is guaranteed to be in $C_1$, knowing only that $a=(0,1)$ and $b=(1,1)$ are in it.</p>", "idea": "<p>K-means clusters are convex cells (intersections of half-planes), so if two points are in a cluster, the whole segment between them is too.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$(0.2,1)=0.8\\,a+0.2\\,b$, a point on the segment $ab$.</li><li>The cell of $C_1$ is $\\{x:g_j(x)\\le0\\}$ with each $g_j$ affine. If $g_j(a)\\le0$ and $g_j(b)\\le0$, then $g_j(0.8a+0.2b)=0.8g_j(a)+0.2g_j(b)\\le0$.</li><li>So $(0.2,1)$ is definitely in $C_1$. Option 3.</li><li>Each other option can be defeated by a choice of centres, e.g. $\\mu_1=(0.5,1)$: for $(1,2)$ take $\\mu_2=(1,3)$ (then $(1,2)$ is closer to $\\mu_2$); for $(0,0)$ take $\\mu_2=(0,-1)$; for $(-1,1)$ take $\\mu_2=(-1,1)$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The same idea says the whole convex hull of the cluster\\'s points stays inside the cluster.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: in 163,585 random centre configurations where $a,b$ shared a cluster, $(0.2,1)$ was never in another cluster; the three counterexamples were checked.</p>", "trap": "Choosing a point because it looks \"close\". Closeness is not a guarantee; being inside the segment is.", "rule": "<p>\"Definitely in the same cluster\" means inside the convex hull of known members.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "With $K=2$, $(0,0),(4,0),(0,4)$ are in one cluster. Which are definitely in it: (a) $(1,1)$, (b) $(2,2)$, (c) $(3,3)$, (d) $(-1,0)$?", "a": "The hull is the triangle $x,y\\ge0,\\ x+y\\le4$. (a) and (b) are in it (b is on the edge); (c) and (d) are outside. Answer: (a) and (b)."}, "optWhy": ["Not guaranteed: it lies outside the segment, and $\\mu_2=(-1,1)$ would capture it.", "Not guaranteed: $\\mu_1=(0.5,1)$, $\\mu_2=(1,3)$ keep $a,b$ in $C_1$ but put $(1,2)$ in $C_2$.", "<b>Correct.</b> It lies on the segment between $a$ and $b$.", "Not guaranteed: $\\mu_1=(0.5,1)$, $\\mu_2=(0,-1)$ keep $a,b$ in $C_1$ but put $(0,0)$ in $C_2$."]}}, {"id": "km12", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "assign", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.3 Cluster assignment, distance, and geometry", "wording": "Consider the one-dimensional dataset $x_1=0,\\ x_2=2,\\ x_3=6,\\ x_4=8$. Suppose K-means is run with $K=2$. At iteration $t$, the centroids are $\\mu_1^t=0,\\ \\mu_2^t=10$. The assignment step produces new assignments $z_i^{t+1}$. Compute $\\sum_{i=1}^4\\big\\|x_i-\\mu^t_{z_i^{t+1}}\\big\\|^2$.", "options": ["4", "8", "24", "44"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $F(z^{t+1},\\mu^t)$: the new labels measured against the old centres.</p>", "idea": "<p>Assign each point to the nearer old centre, then add up squared distances to those old centres. Do not update the centres.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Squared distances: $x=0$: $(0,100)$; $x=2$: $(4,64)$; $x=6$: $(36,16)$; $x=8$: $(64,4)$.</li><li>New labels: $z^{t+1}=(1,1,2,2)$.</li><li>$F=0+4+16+4=24$. Option 3.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>In 1-D the boundary is the midpoint $5$: $0,2$ go to $\\mu_1=0$ (cost $4$) and $6,8$ go to $\\mu_2=10$ (cost $16+4=20$); total $24$. Check with the gap identity: new means $(1,7)$ give $F=4$, and $24=4+2(0-1)^2+2(10-7)^2$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $D=[[0,100],[4,64],[36,16],[64,4]]$, $z=(1,1,2,2)$, old-centre cost $24$; after updating, $\\mu=(1,7)$ and cost $4$.</p>", "trap": "Answering $4$ (the cost after the update), or measuring distances to the new means.", "rule": "<p>Read the superscripts: new labels, old centres means \"assign, do not update\".</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$x=1,3,7,9$ with $\\mu^t=(0,10)$. Find (i) $\\sum\\|x_i-\\mu^t_{z_i^{t+1}}\\|^2$ and (ii) $\\sum\\|x_i-\\mu^{t+1}_{z_i^{t+1}}\\|^2$.", "a": "$z^{t+1}=(1,1,2,2)$. (i) $1+9+9+1=20$. (ii) $\\mu^{t+1}=(2,8)$ gives $1+1+1+1=4$. Gap $20-4=16=2\\cdot4+2\\cdot4$ ✓."}, "optWhy": ["$4$ is the cost after also updating the centres to $(1,7)$ (the question asks for the old centres).", "Does not equal the sum computed from either set of centres.", "<b>Correct.</b> $0+4+16+4=24$.", "Does not equal the sum from the distance table; it is a distractor."]}}, {"id": "km13", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q9", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "objective", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "4.4 Objective, monotonicity, convergence", "tagNote": "Printed data and key disagree: the printed set gives $ab=180$; the key $60$ matches only if the second \"1\" is \"$-1$\". Solved with the key-consistent reading; both values are shown [?].", "wording": "Consider the following dataset in $\\mathbb R$: $\\{-3,1,0,1,4,299,300,301\\}$. Lloyd's algorithm ($K=2$) is run on this dataset with the points $-10$ and $310$ as the initial cluster centers. Let $a,b$ be the final cluster centers on convergence. What is the value of the product $ab$?", "key": {"lo": 60, "hi": 60, "show": "60"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the product $ab$ of the two converged centres.</p>", "idea": "<p>In 1-D the boundary between two centres is their midpoint. Assign by the midpoint, update by the mean, and repeat until nothing moves.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>First boundary: $\\tfrac{-10+310}2=150$. The five small points ($\\le4$) go to cluster 1; $299,300,301$ go to cluster 2.</li><li>Update: $\\mu_2=\\tfrac{299+300+301}3=300$. For cluster 1, the printed set gives $\\mu_1=\\tfrac{-3+1+0+1+4}5=0.6$, so $ab=0.6\\times300=180$.</li><li>Check convergence: the new midpoint is about $150.3$; no point lies between $4$ and $299$, so nothing moves.</li><li>The accepted answer $60$ matches the set only if the second \"1\" is really \"$-1$\": $\\mu_1=\\tfrac{-3-1+0+1+4}5=0.2$ and $ab=0.2\\times300=60$. We use the key-consistent reading.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The $d^2$ table agrees: in pass 1, the point $4$ has $(196,93636)$ and $299$ has $(95481,121)$ to the two centres, so the split is the same.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact fractions): printed data gives $ab=180$ (pass 1 $F$ $889\\to27.2$); the $-1$ reading gives $ab=60$ ($849\\to28.8$). The gap check on the printed data: $889-27.2=861.8=5(0.6+10)^2+3(300-310)^2$ ✓.</p>", "trap": "Taking $-10$ and $310$ as the final centres, or using the mean of all eight points. Also quietly \"fixing\" a typo: here the choice between $180$ and $60$ must be stated.", "rule": "<p>Initial centres far outside two blobs: split at the midpoint, take means, test the new midpoint.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "1-D data $\\{0,2,4,60,62,64,66\\}$, $K=2$, initial centres $10$ and $100$. Find $a,b$ and $ab$.", "a": "The midpoint is $55$: $\\{0,2,4\\}\\mid\\{60,62,64,66\\}$. $a=2$, $b=63$. The new midpoint $32.5$ has no point between, so it is frozen. $ab=126$."}}}, {"id": "km14", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q10", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "objective", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "4.4 Objective, monotonicity, convergence", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key is derived by running Lloyd on the data.", "context": "Data points: $(-2,2),(-2,-2),(0,0),(2,2),(2,-2),(8,2),(8,-2),(9,1),(9,-1)$ (the plotted image of the original question is not reproduced here).", "wording": "Perform K-means clustering with $K=2$ and initial cluster centers at $(0,0)$ and $(6,0)$. What are the final means of clusters after convergence?", "options": ["$(2,0)$ and $(8,0)$", "$(2,0)$ and $(8.5,0)$", "$(0,0)$ and $(8,0)$", "$(0,0)$ and $(8.5,0)$", "None of these"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the final means after convergence.</p>", "idea": "<p>Assign to the nearest centre, take means, and confirm. The initial centres lie on a line and the data is symmetric about the x-axis, so the boundary is a vertical line.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Boundary between $(0,0)$ and $(6,0)$: $12x_1=36$, so $x_1=3$. Points with $x_1\\le2$ (five points) go to cluster 1; points with $x_1\\ge8$ (four points) go to cluster 2.</li><li>Cluster 1 mean: $x=\\tfrac{-2-2+0+2+2}5=0$, $y=\\tfrac{2-2+0+2-2}5=0$, so $(0,0)$.</li><li>Cluster 2 mean: $x=\\tfrac{8+8+9+9}4=8.5$, $y=\\tfrac{2-2+1-1}4=0$, so $(8.5,0)$.</li><li>New boundary $x_1=4.25$; no point lies between $2$ and $8$, so nothing changes. Final: $(0,0)$ and $(8.5,0)$. Option 4.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Distance table, pass 1: squared distances to $(0,0)$ / $(6,0)$: $(2,2)$: $8/20$; $(8,2)$: $68/8$; $(9,1)$: $82/10$. Same split.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: pass 1 $F=68\\to43$ (drop $25=4(8.5-6)^2$); pass 2 changes nothing. On this data the previous question\\'s accepted range $8.18$ to $8.22$ also checks out, so the data transcription looks right.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting that $(0,0)$ is also a data point (a member of cluster 1), or stopping after pass 1 without confirming.", "rule": "<p>Centres on a line plus symmetric data: use the bisector, symmetric coordinates average to zero, then re-test the midpoint.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(-1,1),(-1,-1),(1,1),(1,-1),(9,0),(11,0)$, $K=2$, initial centres $(0,0),(5,0)$. Final means and $F$?", "a": "Boundary $x=2.5$: first four points to cluster 1, the last two to cluster 2. Means $(0,0)$ and $(10,0)$; nothing changes. $F=4\\cdot2+(1+1)=10$."}, "optWhy": ["Cluster 1 has mean $0$, not $2$ (the point $(0,0)$ is a member).", "Same error for cluster 1.", "Cluster 2 has x-mean $8.5$, not $8$.", "<b>Correct.</b> $(0,0)$ and $(8.5,0)$.", "A valid answer exists, so \"none\" is wrong."]}}, {"id": "km15", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "objective", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.4 Objective, monotonicity, convergence", "wording": "In a K-means algorithm with $K=3$, the objective value $F(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}\\|^2$ is recorded after each iteration: Iteration 1: 800, Iteration 2: 500, Iteration 3: 400, Iteration 4: 400, Iteration 5: 400. At which iteration is the algorithm most likely to reach convergence?", "options": ["2", "3", "5", "4"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the iteration at which the algorithm converges, from the recorded $F$ series.</p>", "idea": "<p>$F$ never rises (Lloyd chain). Two equal consecutive values mean the centres have stopped moving, so nothing changes afterwards. The convergence iteration is the last one that still changed something.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$F$ fell strictly in iterations 1, 2 and 3 ($800\\to500\\to400$): each of those passes changed the state.</li><li>$F_3=F_4=F_5=400$: from iteration 3 on the state no longer changes.</li><li>So iteration 3 is the last one that changed anything, the point where the algorithm has reached convergence (key). Iteration 4 is only the first pass that confirms it.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>A similar trace $280,120,88,84,84$ has its last change at iteration 4 and its flat pass at 5.</p>", "verify": "<p>In 6000 random small instances, whenever two consecutive full-pass $F$ values were equal, neither centres nor labels changed afterwards (0 violations).</p>", "trap": "Choosing the first iteration where the value repeats (4) when the key uses the last changing iteration (3). Do not tie the count to $K=3$, and do not read flat $F$ as the global optimum.", "rule": "<p>For an $F$ series, find the first repeated value; the iteration before it (the last change) is the usual answer, but let the options and key fix the convention.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Which $F$ series can occur in Lloyd: (a) $700,400,400,250$ (b) $700,400,300,300,300$ (c) $500,500,300$ (d) $900,600,650$? For a possible one, give its convergence iteration (last changing pass).", "a": "Only (b). (a) and (c) rise or fall after being equal, which contradicts equal $F$ meaning frozen. (d) rises. For (b) the last change is iteration 3."}, "optWhy": ["$F$ still falls from $500$ to $400$ after iteration 2, so it has not converged.", "<b>Correct.</b> The last change happens in iteration 3.", "Already flat for two iterations; nothing new happens at 5.", "The first confirming pass. The key counts the last changing pass, so 3."]}}, {"id": "km16", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 2, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "objective", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "4.4 Objective, monotonicity, convergence", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (statements 1 and 3) is derived by proof [?].", "wording": "Which among the following is true with respect to Lloyd's algorithm?", "options": ["It always converges.", "It may fail to converge for an initialization while it may converge for other initialization.", "It is sensitive to outliers.", "The solution produced is always the optimal."], "key": {"opts": [0, 2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the true statements about Lloyd\\'s algorithm.</p>", "idea": "<p>Lloyd always stops (finite, non-increasing $F$), but at a local fixed point, and the mean makes it sensitive to outliers.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Statement 1 (\"always converges\"): $F$ is non-increasing and every real change lowers it strictly, so no partition repeats; with finitely many partitions a flat pass must come. True (with a fixed tie-break and non-empty clusters).</li><li>Statement 2 (\"may fail to converge for some initialisation\"): every initialisation stops. Initialisation changes where it stops, not whether. False.</li><li>Statement 3 (\"sensitive to outliers\"): the update uses the mean. $\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}$ has mean $2$; adding $100$ gives $\\tfrac{110}6\\approx18.3$. True.</li><li>Statement 4 (\"always optimal\"): it reaches a local fixed point only. A rectangle example stops at $F=16$ while another partition has $F=1$. False.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>K-medians or K-medoids use the median or a data point, which is why they resist outliers; Lloyd uses the mean.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: 6000 random instances all terminated (at most 6 passes) with no violation of the $F$ chain; the outlier mean and the rectangle counterexample are exact.</p>", "trap": "Reading \"converges\" as \"reaches the global optimum\", or reading initialisation-sensitivity as \"may never stop\".", "rule": "<p>\"Always / never / optimal\" statements about Lloyd: it stops, it is local, it is mean-sensitive.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "True or false: (i) $F$ never rises in a pass. (ii) Two initialisations always give the same final $F$. (iii) With $K=2$ Lloyd always takes at most 2 passes. (iv) A far outlier can drag a centre away from the typical points of its cluster.", "a": "(i) True. (ii) False. (iii) False (e.g. points $0..15$ with $K=2$ can take 5 passes). (iv) True."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b> It always terminates.", "False: it terminates for every initialisation.", "<b>True.</b> A single outlier drags a mean.", "False: only a local optimum is guaranteed."]}}, {"id": "km17", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q17", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "objective", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "4.4 Objective, monotonicity, convergence", "tagNote": "Printed list and key disagree: with the printed point $(3,-2)$ the second final mean is $(2.5,-1.5)$ (in no option); the key matches if that point is $(1,-2)$. Solved with the key-consistent reading; the figure is not available [?].", "context": "Dataset 2 (figure not reproduced): $(-3,-1),(-3,4),(-2,3),(1,4),(2,-1),(3,-2)$ as visible in the figure (the list may be incomplete; see the flag on the next question).", "wording": "Perform K-means clustering with $K=2$ and initial cluster centers $(-2,3)$ and $(2,-1)$. What are the final means of the clusters after convergence?", "options": ["$(-1.75,\\,2.5)$ and $(1.5,\\,-1.5)$", "$(-2,\\,3)$ and $(2,\\,-1)$", "$(-1.33,\\,3.66)$ and $(0,\\,-1.33)$", "$(1.33,\\,1)$ and $(2.66,\\,2)$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the final means after convergence, starting at two of the data points.</p>", "idea": "<p>Run Lloyd: assign, take means, confirm. Use two quick filters on the options: a mean must lie inside the convex hull of its cluster, and the final centres must be a fixed point.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Pass 1 (centres $(-2,3),(2,-1)$): squared distances $(-3,-1)$: $17/25$; $(-3,4)$: $2/50$; $(-2,3)$: $0/32$; $(1,4)$: $10/26$; $(2,-1)$: $32/0$; $(3,-2)$: $50/2$. So cluster 1 $=\\{(-3,-1),(-3,4),(-2,3),(1,4)\\}$, cluster 2 $=\\{(2,-1),(3,-2)\\}$.</li><li>Cluster 1 mean: $(\\tfrac{-3-3-2+1}4,\\tfrac{-1+4+3+4}4)=(-1.75,2.5)$.</li><li>Cluster 2 mean on the printed list: $(2.5,-1.5)$, which is in no option. If the last point is $(1,-2)$ instead of $(3,-2)$ the mean is $(1.5,-1.5)$, matching option 1. We use that key-consistent reading.</li><li>Pass 2 with $(-1.75,2.5)$ and $(1.5,-1.5)$: every point keeps its cluster. Converged: option 1.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Hull test: a mean is a convex combination of its members, so it lies inside the hull. $(2.66,2)$ is outside in both readings of the data.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): printed list gives final $(-1.75,2.5),(2.5,-1.5)$; the $(1,-2)$ reading gives $(-1.75,2.5),(1.5,-1.5)$; $F$ goes $31\\to28.75$ and pass 2 changes nothing.</p>", "trap": "Misreading a point from the figure ($3$ vs $1$). When the key matches no mean of the printed data, check the other cluster and use the hull test. Also avoid sending $(-3,-1)$ to cluster 2 by eye.", "rule": "<p>Data plus options that are centres: run one pass, then use the hull and fixed-point tests on the rest.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(0,4),(1,2),(6,0),(6,4)$, $K=2$, initial centres $(0,0)$ and $(6,4)$. Final means and $F$?", "a": "Pass 1: $(1,2),(0,4)\\to$ cluster 1; $(6,0)\\to$ cluster 2. Means $(\\tfrac13,2)$ and $(6,2)$; pass 2 changes nothing. $F=\\tfrac{50}3\\approx16.67$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b> $(-1.75,2.5)$ and $(1.5,-1.5)$ is a fixed point.", "These are only the starting centres; cluster 1\\'s mean is $(-1.75,2.5)\\ne(-2,3)$, so it is not final.", "$(-1.33,3.66)$ is the mean of $(-3,4),(-2,3),(1,4)$, leaving $(-3,-1)$ out; but $(-3,-1)$ is closer to $(-2,3)$ ($17<25$), so it joins cluster 1.", "$(2.66,2)$ lies outside the convex hull of the data, so it cannot be a cluster mean."]}}, {"id": "km18", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q18", "marks": 2, "type": "SA", "concept": "objective", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.4 Objective, monotonicity, convergence", "tagNote": "Counting convention: only passes that changed something are counted (matches the key 1).", "context": "Dataset 2 (figure not reproduced): $(-3,-1),(-3,4),(-2,3),(1,4),(2,-1),(3,-2)$ as visible in the figure (the list may be incomplete; see the flag on the next question).", "wording": "Suppose a new data point $(0,0)$ is introduced to the existing dataset and K-means is performed with the same initial cluster centers. How many iterations would it require to reach convergence?", "key": {"lo": 1, "hi": 1, "show": "1"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the number of iterations to convergence after adding $(0,0)$ to the previous dataset (same initial centres $(-2,3),(2,-1)$).</p>", "idea": "<p>Count the passes that changed something. Adding one point shifts only one cluster mean, so the earlier partition largely survives.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>The new point: squared distance to $(-2,3)$ is $13$ and to $(2,-1)$ is $5$, so it joins cluster 2.</li><li>Pass 1 assignment gives cluster 1 $=\\{(-3,-1),(-3,4),(-2,3),(1,4)\\}$ and cluster 2 $=\\{(2,-1),(1,-2),(0,0)\\}$ (key-consistent reading). Update: $\\mu_1=(-1.75,2.5)$ and $\\mu_2=(1,-1)$.</li><li>Pass 2: $(0,0)$ is $9.3125$ from $\\mu_1$ and $2$ from $\\mu_2$; $(-3,-1)$ is $13.8125$ from $\\mu_1$ and $16$ from $\\mu_2$ (it stays, narrowly); $(1,4)$ stays. No label changes: converged.</li><li>Only pass 1 changed anything, so the count is $1$ (the key counts changing passes; the confirming pass is not counted).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Shortcut: the new point moves only $\\mu_2$ (to about $(1,-1)$), so the only point near the boundary is $(-3,-1)$, and it stays.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): the $(1,-2)$ reading gives $F$ $36\\to31.75$, final $(-1.75,2.5),(1,-1)$; the printed list gives $36\\to34.4167$. Both have a no-change pass 2, so the count is $1$ either way.</p>", "trap": "Counting the confirming pass (answer 2), or thinking one new point changes everything.", "rule": "<p>\"Add a point, how many iterations\": see which single mean it moves, then check which points sit near a boundary.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "In the earlier transfer dataset $(0,0),(0,4),(1,2),(6,0),(6,4)$ with initial centres $(0,0),(6,4)$, add $(2,2)$. Final means, and iterations (changing-pass count and confirming-pass count)?", "a": "$(2,2)$ goes to cluster 1. Final means $(0.75,2)$ and $(6,2)$. Pass 1 changes the state and pass 2 does not: count $1$ (or $2$ if the confirming pass is counted)."}}}, {"id": "km19", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "objective", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.4 Objective, monotonicity, convergence", "wording": "A dataset containing 1,000 points is clustered using the K-means algorithm. Which value of $k$ is most likely to yield the minimum value of the K-means objective function?", "options": ["$k=1$", "$k=10$", "$k=100$", "$k=1000$"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which $k$ gives the smallest objective value $F$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The best achievable $F^*(k)$ never increases when $k$ grows, and $F^*(n)=0$ because every point can be its own centre.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$F^*(k)=\\min_{|M|=k}\\sum_i\\min_{\\mu\\in M}\\|x_i-\\mu\\|^2$ over sets $M$ of $k$ centres.</li><li>Adding a centre can only keep or lower each point\\'s nearest distance, so $F^*(k+1)\\le F^*(k)$.</li><li>At $k=n=1000$, put a centre on each point: $F=0$, the smallest possible value.</li><li>So $F^*(1)\\ge F^*(10)\\ge F^*(100)\\ge F^*(1000)=0$: $k=1000$. Option 4.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Tiny check on $\\{0,1,5\\}$: $k=1$ gives $14$; $k=2$ gives $0.5$; $k=3$ gives $0$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code on 1000 synthetic points: $F$ for $k=1,2,4,10,100,1000$ is $8586.3,4603.0,707.8,382.3,41.3,0.0$, monotone, with exactly $0$ at $k=1000$.</p>", "trap": "Rejecting $k=1000$ as \"overfitting\". The question asks only which $k$ minimises $F$, and $F$ alone is a bad way to choose $k$ (use the elbow idea instead).", "rule": "<p>\"Which $k$ minimises $F$\" means the largest $k$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "1000 points sit on only 3 distinct locations. What is the smallest $k$ with $F^*(k)=0$? Does $k=1000$ also give $0$?", "a": "Smallest $k=3$ (one centre per location). $k=1000$ also gives $0$, so every $k\\ge3$ ties; hence the wording \"most likely\"."}, "optWhy": ["One centre: the largest $F$ (total scatter).", "Larger than $F^*(100)$ and $F^*(1000)$.", "Lower than $k=10$, but still above $0$.", "<b>Correct.</b> Every point has its own centre, so $F=0$."]}}, {"id": "km20", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "tagNote": "The source answer is $(10,10)$, but on the printed data it ranks third by distance; no scoring rule puts it first. Our key is $(13,13)$. It may be a key-copy error [?].", "context": "K-means++ with $k=3$ on the 2-D points $(2,2),(3,3),(4,4),(10,10),(12,12),(13,13)$. The first cluster mean chosen is $(2,2)$.", "wording": "Which point has the highest probability of being chosen as the 2nd cluster mean? Use Manhattan distance to compute the distances.", "options": ["$(4,4)$", "$(12,12)$", "$(10,10)$", "$(13,13)$"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the point most likely to be chosen as the second mean, using Manhattan distance from $\\mu_1=(2,2)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>In K-means++ the chance of choosing a point grows with its score, which increases with its distance from the chosen centres. So the farthest point is the most likely.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Manhattan distances from $(2,2)$: $(3,3)\\to2$, $(4,4)\\to4$, $(10,10)\\to16$, $(12,12)\\to20$, $(13,13)\\to22$.</li><li>Score is an increasing function of the distance (plain distance, its square, or Euclidean squared). With plain distance the sum is $64$ and $\\Pr(13,13)=\\tfrac{22}{64}=\\tfrac{11}{32}$; with squared distance $\\Pr(13,13)=\\tfrac{484}{1160}\\approx0.417$.</li><li>In every reading, $(13,13)$ has the highest probability and $(10,10)$ only the third highest. Option 4.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Ranking only: the options\\' distances are $4,16,20,22$, so the order of probabilities is the same as the order of distances; no normalising is needed.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): plain-distance sum $64$, squared sum $1160$, Euclidean-squared sum $580$; $(13,13)$ wins under all three and probabilities sum to $1$. No reading puts $(10,10)$ first.</p>", "trap": "Measuring distance from the data mean or the origin instead of $\\mu_1$, or trusting the printed key over the data.", "rule": "<p>\"Highest probability for the $j$-th mean\": the point farthest from the centres already chosen.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(1,1),(3,3),(7,7),(9,9)$ with $\\mu_1=(0,0)$ and score equal to the Manhattan distance. Which point is most likely to be chosen, and with what probability?", "a": "Distances $0,2,6,14,18$, sum $40$. The most likely is $(9,9)$ with $\\tfrac{18}{40}=0.45$."}, "optWhy": ["$(4,4)$ is close to $\\mu_1$, so it has a low probability.", "$(12,12)$ is second farthest, so second highest.", "The source marks this, but its distance (16) ranks only third; no scoring rule ranks it first.", "<b>Correct.</b> It is farthest ($D=22$)."]}}, {"id": "km21", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q12", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "assign", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "tagNote": "The source answer $(10,10)$ matches the point $(5,10)$, not the printed $(-5,10)$. Our key is $(3,3)$ for the printed point; whether the copy or the key is wrong is not determined [?].", "context": "K-means++ with $k=3$ on the 2-D points $(2,2),(3,3),(4,4),(10,10),(12,12),(13,13)$. The first cluster mean chosen is $(2,2)$.", "wording": "Assume that $(3,3),\\ (10,10),\\ (12,12)$ are the cluster means. A new data point $(-5,10)$ is introduced. Which cluster mean is the data point $(-5,10)$ closest to using Euclidean distance?", "options": ["$(3,3)$", "$(10,10)$", "$(12,12)$", "None of these"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the cluster mean nearest to $(-5,10)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Nearest centre means the smallest squared Euclidean distance. This is the assign step of Lloyd, not a K-means++ calculation.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>To $(3,3)$: $(-5-3)^2+(10-3)^2=64+49=113$.</li><li>To $(10,10)$: $(-5-10)^2+0=225$.</li><li>To $(12,12)$: $(-5-12)^2+(10-12)^2=289+4=293$.</li><li>The smallest is $113$, so the nearest mean is $(3,3)$. Option 1.</li><li>The source answer is $(10,10)$. That fits a point $(5,10)$ instead (distances $53,25,53$). With $-5$ as printed, $(3,3)$ wins.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The means lie on $y=x$, so $d^2=2(t-m)^2+\\tfrac{(x-y)^2}2$ with $m=\\tfrac{x+y}2=2.5$; the nearest $t$ is the one closest to $2.5$, i.e. $3$. Bisector check: $x+y=5$ is below both $13$ and $15$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $d^2=[113,225,293]$, so $(3,3)$; for $(5,10)$ it is $[53,25,53]$, so $(10,10)$.</p>", "trap": "Misreading the sign of $-5$ (which gives the source answer), or picking $(10,10)$ because the $y$-coordinate matches while ignoring the $x$ gap of $15$.", "rule": "<p>\"Closest mean\": a squared-distance table, or one bisector number when the means are collinear.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Means $(0,0),(6,0),(3,6)$ and a new point $(4,2)$. Nearest mean?", "a": "Squared distances $20,8,17$, so $(6,0)$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct</b> for the printed point: $d^2=113$.", "The source answer; it would be right only for $(5,10)$ (a likely sign slip).", "Farthest of the three ($293$).", "A valid nearest mean exists, so \"none\" is wrong."]}}, {"id": "km22", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 5, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (1 and 4) is derived; statement 4 is a cost judgement and is less certain [?].", "wording": "Consider Lloyd's algorithm used for k-means clustering and choose the correct statements.", "options": ["K-means algorithm may get stuck at local minima.", "It guarantees finding the optimal clustering (global minimum) in every run.", "In practice, $k$ should be as large as possible.", "If the resources are limited and the data set is huge, it will be good to prefer K-means over K-means++."], "key": {"opts": [0, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the correct statements about Lloyd\\'s algorithm and its initialisation.</p>", "idea": "<p>Lloyd reaches a local fixed point only; $F^*(k)$ falls as $k$ grows; K-means++ spends extra work on seeding.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Statement 1 (\"may get stuck at local minima\") is true. Rectangle $(0,0),(4,0),(0,2),(4,2)$ with initial centres $(2,0),(2,2)$ stops at the top/bottom split with $F=16$, while the left/right split has $F=4$.</li><li>Statement 2 (\"guarantees the global minimum in every run\") is false: the same example stops at $F=16$ when the optimum is $4$.</li><li>Statement 3 (\"$k$ should be as large as possible\") is false: $F^*(k)$ keeps falling and is $0$ at $k=n$ (here $20,4,2,0$ for $k=1..4$), which says nothing about a good clustering.</li><li>Statement 4 (\"with limited resources and a huge dataset, prefer K-means over K-means++\") is true on the exam\\'s reading: K-means++ seeding needs $K-1$ sequential rounds, each updating the distances of all $n$ points, about $O(nKd)$ extra work, while uniform random seeding needs no pass over the data.</li><li>Answer: statements 1 and 4. Statement 4 is a judgement about cost and is less certain.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Wording check: \"guarantees\", \"always\" and \"as large as possible\" are absolute claims and usually false; \"may\" needs only one example.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): the rectangle run ends at $F=16$ and brute force gives $F^*(1,2,3,4)=20,4,2,0$. Statement 4 is a cost argument and cannot be checked by code; a key that rejects it would argue that K-means++ can cut the total number of Lloyd iterations [?].</p>", "trap": "Equating \"converges\" with \"optimal\", choosing a huge $k$ because $F$ is smaller, or rejecting statement 4 on the slogan that K-means++ is always better.", "rule": "<p>Absolute wording (\"guarantees\", \"always\", \"as large as possible\") deserves a counterexample; cost statements deserve a work count.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Which are true about K-means++: (a) a chosen point can be chosen again; (b) a point\\'s probability is proportional to its squared distance from its nearest chosen centre; (c) Lloyd is still run afterwards; (d) it guarantees the global optimum?", "a": "(b) and (c). (a) is false because a chosen point has distance $0$ and so score $0$. (d) is false: a poor run can still stop at $F=16$ with small probability."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b> Local minima are possible.", "False: no global guarantee.", "False: larger $k$ only lowers $F$, it does not make clustering better.", "<b>True</b> on the cost reading: plain K-means has the cheaper start."]}}, {"id": "km23", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "tagNote": "The score rule is not printed in this copy. The key $0.25$ comes from the squared rule [?].", "wording": "Consider K-means++ with $k=2$ on a dataset of 7 points: $D=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_7\\}$. The point $x_7$ has been selected as the first mean: $\\mu_1=x_7$. The Euclidean distances from the remaining points to $\\mu_1$ are:<table><tr><th>Point</th><th>$d(x_i,\\mu_1)$</th></tr><tr><td>$x_1$</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>$x_2$</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>$x_3$</td><td>$\\sqrt2$</td></tr><tr><td>$x_4$</td><td>$\\sqrt3$</td></tr><tr><td>$x_5$</td><td>$\\sqrt5$</td></tr><tr><td>$x_6$</td><td>1</td></tr></table>What is the probability of choosing $x_2$ as the second mean?", "key": {"lo": 0.25, "hi": 0.25, "show": "0.25"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\Pr(\\mu_2=x_2)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>In K-means++ a point is chosen with probability $D^2(x)/\\sum D^2$, where $D$ is its distance to the nearest chosen centre. The already chosen point has score $0$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Squared distances: $x_1:1$, $x_2:4$, $x_3:2$, $x_4:3$, $x_5:5$, $x_6:1$, $x_7:0$.</li><li>Sum $=1+4+2+3+5+1=16$.</li><li>$\\Pr(x_2)=\\tfrac4{16}=0.25$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>If the score were the plain distance, the sum would be $9.3824$ and $\\Pr(x_2)=2/9.3824\\approx0.2132$, which does not match the accepted answer; so the key uses the squared rule.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): the probabilities $\\tfrac1{16},\\tfrac4{16},\\tfrac2{16},\\tfrac3{16},\\tfrac5{16},\\tfrac1{16}$ sum to $1$; $\\Pr(x_2)=\\tfrac14$.</p>", "trap": "Normalising by plain distances, or squaring $\\sqrt2$ incorrectly. Remember $x_7$ is already a mean and has score $0$.", "rule": "<p>\"Probability of choosing $x$\": square, add, divide; a chosen point scores $0$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "K-means++, $k=2$, six points; the other five are at distances $1,1,\\sqrt2,\\sqrt3,2$ from $\\mu_1$. Probability that the $\\sqrt3$ point is the second mean (squared rule)?", "a": "Scores $1,1,2,3,4$, sum $11$, so $\\tfrac3{11}\\approx0.273$."}}}, {"id": "km24", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "context": "K-means++ with $k=3$ on the points $(0,1),(1,0),(1,2),(2,1),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2)$. The first mean is $\\mu_1^0=(2,1)$. Suppose the point with the highest score is chosen as the second mean $\\mu_2^0$. Scores use squared distance.", "wording": "What is $\\mu_2^0$?", "options": ["$(0,1)$", "$(2,3)$", "$(3,2)$", "$(2,4)$"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the second mean when the highest-scoring point is chosen.</p>", "idea": "<p>\"Highest score is chosen\" is deterministic: $\\mu_2=\\arg\\max_x\\|x-\\mu_1\\|^2$. No probabilities are needed.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Squared distances from $(2,1)$: $(0,1):4$, $(1,0):2$, $(1,2):2$, $(2,3):4$, $(2,4):9$, $(3,2):2$.</li><li>The maximum is $9$, only at $(2,4)$. No tie. $\\mu_2^0=(2,4)$. Option 4.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Only the four options need checking: $4,4,2,9$. Plain distance or Manhattan distance also pick $(2,4)$, so the answer does not depend on the rule.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $D^2=[4,2,2,0,4,9,2]$, argmax is $(2,4)$.</p>", "trap": "Treating \"highest score\" as \"highest probability\" and summing all scores (wasted time), or reading $\\mu_1$ wrongly (for example as the data mean).", "rule": "<p>\"Highest score is chosen\": one argmax of $D^2$, no normalising.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(4,0),(0,5),(3,3)$ with $\\mu_1=(0,0)$ and score $D^2$. Which point is $\\mu_2$ (highest score), and what is its score?", "a": "Scores $0,1,1,16,25,18$, so $\\mu_2=(0,5)$ with score $25$."}, "optWhy": ["Score $4$, not the maximum.", "Score $4$, not the maximum.", "Score $2$.", "<b>Correct.</b> Score $9$, the largest."]}}, {"id": "km25", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q16", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "context": "K-means++ with $k=3$ on the points $(0,1),(1,0),(1,2),(2,1),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2)$. The first mean is $\\mu_1^0=(2,1)$. Suppose the point with the highest score is chosen as the second mean $\\mu_2^0$. Scores use squared distance.", "wording": "After selecting the second mean, which point has the lowest probability of being chosen as the 3rd cluster mean? Use squared distance to calculate the scores.", "options": ["$(1,0)$", "$(2,3)$", "$(3,2)$", "$(1,2)$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the point least likely to become the third mean, given $\\mu_1=(2,1)$ and $\\mu_2=(2,4)$ (from the previous question).</p>", "idea": "<p>With two centres chosen, each point\\'s score is the squared distance to its nearest centre: $s=\\min(d_1^2,d_2^2)$. The smallest score gives the lowest probability.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Scores $(\\min D^2)$: $(0,1)$: $\\min(4,13)=4$; $(1,0)$: $\\min(2,17)=2$; $(1,2)$: $\\min(2,5)=2$; $(2,3)$: $\\min(4,1)=1$; $(3,2)$: $\\min(2,5)=2$; the two chosen centres score $0$.</li><li>Sum $=11$, so the probabilities are $\\tfrac4{11},\\tfrac2{11},\\tfrac2{11},\\tfrac1{11},\\tfrac2{11}$ for the five candidates.</li><li>The smallest is $\\tfrac1{11}$, at $(2,3)$ (it is only $1$ away from $\\mu_2$). Option 2.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Only the options matter: nearest-centre squared distances $2,1,2,2$ for $(1,0),(2,3),(3,2),(1,2)$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): $s=[4,2,2,0,1,0,2]$, sum $11$, probabilities sum to $1$. The sum $11$ equals the cost $F$ of nearest assignment to the two centres.</p>", "trap": "Using the distance to only $\\mu_1$ (then three points tie and $(2,3)$ looks large), or counting the two chosen centres as candidates (their score is $0$ and they cannot be chosen again).", "rule": "<p>\"Third mean\": each point\\'s score is its distance to the nearest of all chosen centres.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Continue the earlier transfer: $\\mu_1=(0,0),\\mu_2=(0,5)$, points $(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(4,0),(0,5),(3,3)$, score $D^2$. Probability that $(4,0)$ is the third centre?", "a": "Scores $0,1,1,16,0,13$ (for $(3,3)$: $\\min(18,13)=13$), sum $31$, so $\\tfrac{16}{31}\\approx0.516$."}, "optWhy": ["Score $2$.", "<b>Correct.</b> Score $1$, the smallest.", "Score $2$.", "Score $2$."]}}, {"id": "km26", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q9", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "tagNote": "The score rule is not printed. The accepted range $0.07$ to $0.11$ matches the plain-distance rule; the usual squared rule would give $0.0303$ [?].", "wording": "K-means++ is run on the following dataset with $K=2$. The plotted points are: $(2,5),\\ (0,3),\\ (2,4),\\ (1,1),\\ (1,0)$. Use the point $(1,1)$ as the first cluster mean. Then find the probability of the point that has the least chance of being chosen as the second cluster mean. Use Euclidean distance to calculate the distance.", "key": {"lo": 0.07, "hi": 0.11, "show": "0.07 to 0.11"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the probability of the least likely point, given $\\mu_1=(1,1)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The chance of a point grows with its score, so the least likely point is the nearest one. The accepted range shows that the score here is the plain Euclidean distance, not its square.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Distances from $(1,1)$: $(2,5)\\to\\sqrt{17}=4.1231$; $(0,3)\\to\\sqrt5=2.2361$; $(2,4)\\to\\sqrt{10}=3.1623$; $(1,0)\\to1$; the centre itself $\\to0$.</li><li>Sum $=1+\\sqrt5+\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{17}=10.5215$.</li><li>The smallest distance is $1$, at $(1,0)$. $\\Pr(1,0)=\\tfrac1{10.5215}=0.0950$, inside $0.07$ to $0.11$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Rule check: with squared distances the scores are $17,5,10,0,1$ with sum $33$, and $\\Pr(1,0)=\\tfrac1{33}=0.0303$, outside the accepted range. So the key uses plain distance. In both rules $(1,0)$ is the least likely.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: plain-distance probabilities $[0.3919,0.2125,0.3006,0,0.0950]$ (sum $1$); squared-distance probabilities $[0.5152,0.1515,0.3030,0,0.0303]$.</p>", "trap": "Applying the usual squared rule by habit and getting $0.0303$. When the rule is not stated, compute both and keep the one that fits the accepted range.", "rule": "<p>Number asked, rule unstated: compute with $D$ and $D^2$ and match the answer range.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(3,4),(6,8),(1,0)$, $\\mu_1=(0,0)$, $K=2$. The least likely point\\'s probability with the plain-distance rule? With the squared rule?", "a": "Plain: $D=0,5,10,1$, sum $16$, so $\\tfrac1{16}=0.0625$. Squared: sum $126$, so $\\tfrac1{126}\\approx0.0079$."}}}, {"id": "km27", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "wording": "How does the K-means++ algorithm enhance the initialization process as compared to the standard K-means algorithm?", "options": ["It ensures the algorithm converges in a fixed number of iterations.", "It improves centroid initialization by spreading them out, leading to better-separated clusters.", "It automatically calculates the best value of $K$.", "It removes the need to run the algorithm multiple times."], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> what K-means++ improves over standard initialisation.</p>", "idea": "<p>K-means++ changes only how the starting centres are chosen: each new centre is sampled in proportion to its score, which tends to spread the centres out. The rest is ordinary Lloyd.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Test each claim against \"what does K-means++ change, and what does it not?\".</li><li>Only the claim about spreading the starting centres follows from the definition. The others promise a fixed iteration count, an automatic $K$, or no need for repeated runs, none of which the method gives.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Words like \"ensures\", \"automatically\" and \"removes the need\" signal over-claims.</p>", "verify": "<p>Only option 2 avoids a guarantee that K-means++ does not provide; it matches the source key.</p>", "trap": "Reading \"usually converges faster\" as \"converges in a fixed number of iterations\", or \"better start\" as \"one run is enough\".", "rule": "<p>K-means++ is a sampling rule for the starting centres only: no guarantee on $K$, iterations or the global optimum.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "True or false: in K-means++ a data point that has already been chosen as a centre can never be chosen again (all points distinct).", "a": "True. A chosen point has distance $0$, so score $0$ and probability $0$."}, "optWhy": ["False: the iteration count still depends on Lloyd and the data; there is no fixed count.", "<b>Correct.</b> Sampling by score favours far-away points, so the centres are spread out.", "False: $K$ is an input. Choosing the best $K$ is a separate problem (elbow).", "False: it is randomised and still ends at a local optimum, so repeated runs can still help."]}}, {"id": "km28", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (2, 3, 4) is derived. Statement 2 assumes the squared rule; statement 3 assumes the confirming pass is not counted [?].", "wording": "Consider the dataset $(0,0),\\ (0,2),\\ (2,0),\\ (2,2)$. K-means++ is run with $K=2$, with the first cluster center chosen as $(0,0)$. The score of a point $x$ is based on its distance from the initial cluster center. Which statements are correct?", "options": ["The points $(0,2),(2,0),(2,2)$ have equal probability of being chosen as the second cluster center.", "The probability of selecting $(2,2)$ as the second cluster center is twice that of selecting $(0,2)$.", "If $(2,2)$ is chosen as the second cluster center, the algorithm will always converge in one iteration.", "The tie-breaking rule will affect the final cluster centers."], "key": {"opts": [1, 2, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which of four statements about K-means++ on the corners of a square are correct.</p>", "idea": "<p>With $\\mu_1=(0,0)$ the scores are $D^2=4,4,8$. If $\\mu_2=(2,2)$, the points $(0,2)$ and $(2,0)$ lie on the bisector $x+y=2$ and tie between the two centres, so the tie-break matters.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Scores: $(0,2):4$, $(2,0):4$, $(2,2):8$; sum $16$. Probabilities $\\tfrac14,\\tfrac14,\\tfrac12$.</li><li>Statement 1 (equal probabilities): $\\tfrac14,\\tfrac14,\\tfrac12$ are not equal. False.</li><li>Statement 2 (\"twice\"): $\\tfrac12=2\\cdot\\tfrac14$. True under the squared rule (plain distance would give a ratio of $\\sqrt2$).</li><li>Statement 3: take centres $(0,0),(2,2)$. Both $(0,2)$ and $(2,0)$ are tied. For all four tie-breaks the new centres are $(\\tfrac23,\\tfrac23)\\&(2,2)$, $(0,0)\\&(\\tfrac43,\\tfrac43)$, $(0,1)\\&(2,1)$ or $(1,0)\\&(1,2)$, and in each case the next assignment changes nothing. One iteration suffices (if the confirming pass is not counted).</li><li>Statement 4: the four tie-breaks give different final centres and different costs ($\\tfrac{16}3$ or $4$). True.</li><li>Answer: statements 2, 3 and 4.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Symmetry shows the same thing: the two tied points go to the same cluster (cost $\\tfrac{16}3$) or to different clusters (cost $4$).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): $D^2$ probabilities $0.25,0.25,0.5$; plain distance $0.2929,0.2929,0.4142$. All four tie-breaks end with no change in pass 2; $F=\\tfrac{16}3,\\tfrac{16}3,4,4$.</p>", "trap": "Saying $(2,2)$ is \"just as far\" as the others (its distance is $2\\sqrt2$), assuming ties never matter, or counting the confirming pass for statement 3.", "rule": "<p>Symmetric data and two centres on a diagonal: points on the bisector tie, and the tie-break can change the result.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Square corners $(0,0),(0,4),(4,0),(4,4)$ with centres $(0,0)$ and $(4,4)$. How many different final $F$ values arise over the tie-breaks, and what are they?", "a": "Two: $\\tfrac{64}3\\approx21.33$ (both tied points in one cluster) and $16$ (split). This is the earlier case scaled by $4$."}, "optWhy": ["False: the probabilities are $\\tfrac14,\\tfrac14,\\tfrac12$.", "<b>True</b> (squared rule).", "<b>True</b> if only changing passes are counted; with the other convention it would take two.", "<b>True:</b> the final centres and $F$ depend on how the ties are broken."]}}, {"id": "km29", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "kpp", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "4.5 K-means++ initialisation", "tagNote": "The score rule is not printed. The key $0.5$ comes from the squared rule; plain distance gives $0.4142$ [?].", "wording": "Consider the following five data-points in a clustering problem with three clusters ($k=3$): $(-1,0),\\ (0,0),\\ (1,1),\\ (2,1),\\ (1,-1)$. K-means++ is used to initialize the three means. The first mean is $\\mu_1=(0,0)$ and the second mean is $\\mu_2=(1,1)$. Find the probability of choosing $(1,-1)$ as the next center.", "key": {"lo": 0.5, "hi": 0.5, "show": "0.5"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\Pr((1,-1)\\text{ is the third mean})$.</p>", "idea": "<p>With two centres chosen, the score is the squared distance to the nearest centre, $s=\\min(d_1^2,d_2^2)$, and $\\Pr=s/\\sum s$; chosen points score $0$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Scores: $(-1,0)$: $\\min(1,5)=1$; $(0,0)$: $0$; $(1,1)$: $0$; $(2,1)$: $\\min(5,1)=1$; $(1,-1)$: $\\min(2,4)=2$.</li><li>Sum $=1+0+0+1+2=4$.</li><li>$\\Pr(1,-1)=\\tfrac24=0.5$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Cost view: nearest assignment gives cluster $\\{(-1,0),(0,0),(1,-1)\\}$ with cost $3$ and $\\{(1,1),(2,1)\\}$ with cost $1$; $F=4=\\sum s$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code (exact): $s=[1,0,0,1,2]$, sum $4$, probability $\\tfrac12$. The plain-distance rule would give $0.4142$, not the key.</p>", "trap": "Using only $\\mu_1$ (giving $0.2$) or only $\\mu_2$ (giving $0.333$), or giving the chosen centres a non-zero score.", "rule": "<p>\"Next center\" with centres already chosen: nearest-centre squared distances, then normalise.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Points $(0,0),(2,0),(0,2),(3,3),(6,0)$ with $\\mu_1=(0,0)$ and $\\mu_2=(6,0)$, squared rule. Probability that $(3,3)$ is the third centre?", "a": "Scores $0,4,4,18,0$ (for $(3,3)$: $\\min(18,18)$), sum $26$, so $\\tfrac{18}{26}=\\tfrac9{13}\\approx0.692$."}}}], "test": [{"id": "t1", "concept": "objective", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Cluster $\\{1,3\\}$ with centre $2$ and cluster $\\{10,12\\}$ with centre $11$. $F$?", "num": {"ans": 4, "tol": 0.001, "show": "4"}, "explain": "$1+1+1+1=4$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t2", "concept": "update", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Cluster points $(1,2),(3,4),(5,0)$. First coordinate of the new centre?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "$(1+3+5)/3=3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t3", "concept": "assign", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Points $0$ and $10$; centres $4$ and $9$. Which cluster does $x=7$ join?", "options": [{"t": "Centre 4 (distance 3)", "ok": false, "why": "$|7-4|=3>2$."}, {"t": "Centre 9 (distance 2)", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: $2<3$."}, {"t": "Neither", "ok": false, "why": "Every point is assigned."}, {"t": "Both", "ok": false, "why": "Hard assignment: one cluster only."}]}, {"id": "t4", "concept": "kpp", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Chosen centre $(0,0)$; points $(3,0)$ and $(0,4)$ remain, nothing else. Probability that K-means++ picks $(0,4)$?", "num": {"ans": 0.64, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.64"}, "explain": "$D^2=9$ and $16$; $16/25=0.64$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t5", "concept": "objective", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "As $K$ increases (best clustering for each $K$), the optimal $F$", "options": [{"t": "increases", "ok": false, "why": "More centres can only help."}, {"t": "decreases (non-increasing) and is $0$ at $K=n$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "stays constant", "ok": false, "why": "It changes."}, {"t": "is minimised at $K=1$", "ok": false, "why": "$K=1$ gives the largest $F$."}]}, {"id": "t6", "concept": "objective", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which is true of Lloyd\\'s algorithm?", "options": [{"t": "It always finds the global minimum of $F$", "ok": false, "why": "Not guaranteed."}, {"t": "$F$ never increases and it stops in finitely many steps, possibly at a local minimum", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "It may cycle forever", "ok": false, "why": "It cannot cycle: $F$ strictly drops unless assignments are unchanged."}, {"t": "It does not depend on the starting centres", "ok": false, "why": "It does depend."}]}, {"id": "t7", "concept": "calc", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $1,2,6,7$, centres $1,2$. $F$ after convergence?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "Final clusters $\\{1,2\\},\\{6,7\\}$, centres $1.5,6.5$: $F=1$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t8", "concept": "update", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $(0,0),(0,2),(4,0),(4,2)$; clusters left pair and right pair. $F$?", "num": {"ans": 4, "tol": 0.001, "show": "4"}, "explain": "Each point is $1$ from its centre: $4\\times1=4$.", "hint": ""}], "checklist": [{"id": "c1", "t": "I can run two passes of Lloyd by hand with a distance table."}, {"id": "c2", "t": "I know F never rises and equal consecutive F means converged."}, {"id": "c3", "t": "I can compute a K-means++ probability: s=D² to nearest centre, divide by the sum, chosen points score 0."}, {"id": "c4", "t": "I know K-means++ gives a spread start, not a global optimum or a choice of K."}], "formulas": [{"name": "Objective", "tex": "F=\\sum_i\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}\\|^2", "note": "Sum of squared distances to the assigned centre."}, {"name": "Update", "tex": "\\mu_k=\\frac1{|C_k|}\\sum_{x\\in C_k}x", "note": "Mean of the members."}, {"name": "K-means++ probability", "tex": "\\Pr(x)=\\frac{D(x)^2}{\\sum_{x'}D(x')^2}", "note": "D is the distance to the nearest chosen centre."}]};